Local 32 MiB `httptest` CDN fixture, 20 runs, 4 workers:

| Strategy | Chunk | P50 | P95 | Requests/run | Failures | SHA-256 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| HEAD + fixed Range baseline | 1 MiB | 124.9921 ms | 154.4273 ms | 33 | 0/20 | `05f052c8f6da8ee5228ec291820b559c4be183773b9e97a6b82e30dacff85dd3` |
| Adaptive optimized | 4 MiB | 54.8062 ms | 138.6823 ms | 9 | 0/20 | `05f052c8f6da8ee5228ec291820b559c4be183773b9e97a6b82e30dacff85dd3` |

P50 improvement: `(124.9921 - 54.8062) / 124.9921 * 100% = 56.15%`.
P95 improvement: 10.21%. Request reduction: 72.73%.

# Skipped checks

- Public CDN performance probe was skipped to avoid network variance and public request budget consumption.
- Peak process working-set was not captured by this local fixture; allocation evidence for completed-state warm resume was 33.57 MiB/op and 97 allocs/op.

# Spec consistency

- Output SHA-256 is identical between baseline and optimized paths.
- URL, size, ETag/Last-Modified and chunk parameters still gate resume reuse.
- Range merge writes each original chunk at its original offset and records per-chunk hashes.
- Range ignored and short/mismatched range responses use the existing full-GET fallback.
- Explicit `RangeChunkSize` overrides remain supported; only the production default delegates to adaptive 1/4/8 MiB selection.

# Known limitations and risks

- Performance percentage is from a deterministic local 32 MiB fixture, not a guarantee for every public CDN route.
- Adaptive thresholds are static size tiers; future network-history feedback can refine them without changing current semantics.

# Conclusion

Verification passed. P50 improved by 56.15%, P95 by 10.21%, and request count fell from 33 to 9 while output SHA-256 remained identical.
